## Proof

**Setup.** Place the regular tetrahedron in coordinates using the standard inscribed-in-cube construction:

$$A=(1,1,1),\quad B=(1,-1,-1),\quad C=(-1,1,-1),\quad D=(-1,-1,1).$$

Every pair of vertices differs in exactly two coordinates by $2$, so each edge has length $2\sqrt{2}$; this is a regular tetrahedron.

**Midpoints and centroids.**

$$M=\frac{A+B}{2}=(1,0,0),\qquad N=\frac{A+C}{2}=(0,1,0).$$

$$P=\frac{A+C+D}{3}=\left(-\tfrac13,\tfrac13,\tfrac13\right),\qquad Q=\frac{A+B+D}{3}=\left(\tfrac13,-\tfrac13,\tfrac13\right).$$

**Direction vectors.**

$$\vec{MP}=P-M=\left(-\tfrac43,\tfrac13,\tfrac13\right),\qquad \vec{NQ}=Q-N=\left(\tfrac13,-\tfrac43,\tfrac13\right).$$

**Dot product and norms.**

$$\vec{MP}\cdot\vec{NQ}=\left(-\tfrac43\right)\!\left(\tfrac13\right)+\left(\tfrac13\right)\!\left(-\tfrac43\right)+\left(\tfrac13\right)\!\left(\tfrac13\right)=-\tfrac49-\tfrac49+\tfrac19=-\tfrac79.$$

$$|\vec{MP}|^2=\tfrac{16}{9}+\tfrac19+\tfrac19=\tfrac{18}{9}=2,\qquad |\vec{NQ}|^2=\tfrac19+\tfrac{16}{9}+\tfrac19=\tfrac{18}{9}=2.$$

**Angle.** Let $\theta$ be the angle between the two lines (taken as the acute angle). Since the dot product is negative, the angle between the directed vectors is obtuse, and the acute angle between the lines satisfies

$$\cos\theta=\frac{|\vec{MP}\cdot\vec{NQ}|}{|\vec{MP}|\,|\vec{NQ}|}=\frac{\tfrac79}{\sqrt2\cdot\sqrt2}=\frac{7}{18}.$$

Therefore,

$$\boxed{\theta=\arccos\frac{7}{18}.}$$

